WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.684
Module six turns sparse sensor data into a full picture of the network, now and in the near future.

2
00:00:06.584 --> 00:00:09.675
Traffic state estimation is like weather forecasting.

3
00:00:09.675 --> 00:00:13.174
A few detectors and probe vehicles give sparse observations.

4
00:00:13.174 --> 00:00:20.348
A flow model fills the gaps, and a filter, such as a Kalman filter, blends the two into speeds and densities on every link.

5
00:00:21.248 --> 00:00:23.237
Prediction comes in two families.

6
00:00:23.237 --> 00:00:28.179
Model-based methods use flow physics and explain themselves, but need calibration.

7
00:00:28.179 --> 00:00:35.412
Data-driven methods, such as LSTMs and graph neural networks, learn patterns from history but need large clean datasets.

8
00:00:36.312 --> 00:00:45.460
Hybrid and physics-informed approaches combine both: a network learns what the physical model gets wrong, or is trained to respect conservation of vehicles.

9
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.802
They need less data and produce fewer impossible answers.

10
00:00:49.702 --> 00:00:50.792
Evaluate honestly.

11
00:00:50.792 --> 00:01:00.720
Report errors by forecast horizon, always compare with simple baselines such as yesterday at the same time, and test on incidents and on months the model never saw.

12
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:05.106
And remember: a forecast is only useful if it arrives in time.

13
00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:10.110
The prediction horizon must be longer than the time it takes to compute it and act on it.
